<h2>Subcontractor coordination</h2>

<p>No construction company operates alone. Electricians, plumbers, plasterers, painters, concreters. Every build involves a network of subcontractors who need to show up at the right time, in the right order, with the right information.</p>

<p>When coordination is handled through phone calls and text messages, things go wrong. The sparky shows up on Tuesday but the frames are not ready until Thursday. The painter arrives before the plaster has dried. The concreter was never told the site access changed. Each miscommunication costs time and money, and the client sees it all.</p>

<p>A CRM with builder project management features lets you manage subcontractor scheduling alongside your client pipeline. You can tag subcontractors in your contact database, assign them to specific projects, and set up automated notifications when their stage is approaching. "Heads up, framing on the Smith project will be complete by Friday. You are scheduled to start plumbing rough-in on Monday." The sub confirms, and everyone stays on the same page.</p>

<p>This is not full-blown project management software with Gantt charts and resource levelling. For most residential builders and small commercial operators, that level of complexity is overkill. What you need is simple, reliable coordination, the same follow-up principles that apply to <a href="/blog/marketing-automation-getting-started">marketing automation</a> applied to your project workflow. Trigger the right message to the right person at the right time, and let the system handle the rest.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Sub database.</strong> Keep every subcontractor's details, specialities, availability notes, and past project history in one place.</li>
  <li><strong>Stage-based notifications.</strong> When you mark a milestone complete, the next trade in the sequence gets an automatic heads-up.</li>
  <li><strong>Confirmation tracking.</strong> You can see at a glance which subs have confirmed and which have not, so you can chase the ones who are slow to respond before it becomes a problem on site.</li>
</ul>

