<h2>What to look for in a construction CRM</h2>

<p>The construction industry does not need a CRM built for tech startups or enterprise sales teams. You need something that works for a business where the team is on site all day, decisions happen fast, and the "office" is often the front seat of a ute.</p>

<p>Here is what matters:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Mobile-first design.</strong> If you cannot use it on your phone between site visits, it is useless. Everything, leads, quotes, pipelines, messages, should be accessible from a mobile app.</li>
  <li><strong>Built-in SMS and email.</strong> Construction clients respond to texts far more reliably than emails. Your CRM should send both without needing a separate tool.</li>
  <li><strong>Pipeline customisation.</strong> You need to set up stages that match your actual workflow, not a generic sales process. Enquiry, site visit, quote sent, follow-up, won, and lost at minimum.</li>
  <li><strong>Automation without complexity.</strong> You should be able to set up a follow-up sequence in minutes, not days. If it requires a developer to configure, it is the wrong tool.</li>
  <li><strong>All-in-one capability.</strong> The last thing a builder needs is five different logins for five different tools. CRM, email, SMS, invoicing triggers, and reputation management should live under one roof.</li>
</ul>

<p>Professional service businesses of all kinds face similar decisions. Whether you are a <a href="/blog/marketing-for-personal-trainers">personal trainer building a client base</a> or a <a href="/blog/marketing-for-law-firms">law firm managing consultations</a>, the core requirements are the same: capture leads, follow up fast, and keep clients informed.</p>

